## 4.2. Population initialization
A novel load balancing initialization rule has been developed based on the proposed CEM encoding operator with five levels. Initially, job priorities are randomly assigned and the job operations are sorted. The key concept is to prioritize operations of jobs with higher priority, assigning them to the same factory, workshop, and machine as the preceding operation to minimize inter-machine, inter-workshop, and inter-factory transportation. Alternatively, if needed, cumulative processing times for machines, workshops, and factories are calculated. Subsequently, machines, workshops, and factories are selected for each operation in sequence based on the minimum cumulative processing time. The specific steps are outlined below, with the core pseudocode of the initialization method detailed in Algorithm 1.
Step 1. Set the total job level as "pri" and randomly assign priorities to the jobs.
Step 2. There are a total of n jobs, pri level, set the range of random number is $\pmb { n } + ( p r i - 1 ) + . . . + 1$ , if the selected random number r belongs to 1\~n, then the operaions of the r-rd job is sorted, if r is >n, according to the number obtained based on r-n., judge the job level t, randomly select a job with a level t for sorting, until all operations are arranged As mentioned above, jobs with high priority will be scheduled for processing first to optimize the weighted average time ofjob priorities. For example, there are 10 jobs with 4 levels and level 1 is the highest. Set the range of random numbers to 10+3 + 2 + 1, the random number in 1\~10 represents the job 1\~10, in 11\~13 select the job with level 1, 14\~15 select the job with level 2, and 16 select the job with level 3. In this way, jobs with higher priorities can be sequenced earlier.
Step 3. If the random number is 1–10, then assign the operation of the corresponding job according to the random number. If a gene of the chromosome (representing an operation) represents the first operation of a job, allocate a factory, workshop, and machine to minimize the cumulative time for this operation; otherwise, proceed to step 4.
Step 4. If a job shares the same factory, workshop, and machine as a previous job, assign the same factory, workshop, and machine to this job, otherwise, assign the factory, workshop, and machine with the minimum cumulative time.
